NAME
       ip - show and change network interfaces, addresses and routes

SYNOPSIS
       ip [-4 | -6] [-br] OBJECT [COMMAND [ARGUMENT...]]

       OBJECT := address (a, addr) | link (l) | route (r) | neighbour (n)

DESCRIPTION
       ip shows the network state of the machine as the kernel sees it now,
       and can change it. Objects and commands may be shortened (ip a, ip r).

       Changes made with ip are temporary: they are lost when the interface
       goes down, when NetworkManager reapplies its connection, or at a
       reboot. To configure an address or route for good, use nmcli (or
       nmtui), which saves NetworkManager connection profiles. Use ip to look
       and to check.

   ip address
       ip address [show [dev] IFACE]
              List the interfaces with their state and addresses. An inet
              line is an IPv4 address with its prefix (/24), inet6 an IPv6
              address; scope link marks the automatic fe80:: address.

       ip address add ADDRESS/PREFIX dev IFACE
       ip address del ADDRESS/PREFIX dev IFACE
              Add or remove an address (temporary, root only).

   ip link
       ip link [show]
              List the interfaces with their MAC address and whether they
              are UP.

       ip link set [dev] IFACE up|down
              Bring an interface up or down (root only).

   ip route
       ip route [show]
              The routing table. The default line names the gateway that
              traffic for other networks goes to:

                  default via 192.168.50.254 dev enp1s0 proto static metric 100

   ip neighbour
       ip neighbour [show]
              The ARP (IPv4) and neighbour (IPv6) cache: addresses on the
              local network and their MAC addresses.

OPTIONS
       -4, -6 Show only IPv4 or only IPv6.

       -br, -brief
              One short line per interface: name, state, addresses.

       -s     Add statistics (packets, errors).

       -c, -color
              Colour the output.

EXAMPLES
       Which addresses does this machine have?

           ip -br address

       Details of one interface:

           ip addr show enp1s0

       What is the default gateway?

           ip route

       Only the IPv6 addresses:

           ip -6 addr show enp1s0

       After an nmcli change, check that it is in effect:

           nmcli connection up enp1s0
           ip addr show enp1s0

SEE ALSO
       nmcli(1), ping(8), ss(8), hostnamectl(1)
